  "descriptions": {
    "preferred": "Probably a cross between Acer palmatum and A. circinatum, this cultivar has sevento nine-lobed circular leaves with broadly ovate lobes and pointed tips. The stiff stout leafstalks are red. The leaves are dark green during the summer, becoming green with bronze and purple tones in late summer. In the fall, this German cultivar comes into its own, turning an outstanding orange. It is very vigorous, densely branched, upright, wide-spreading, and reaching 16-20 ft. (5-6 m) tall and about as wide. The name means \"autumn fire.\""
  },
